      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues to explore the concept of differences that we began in Chapter 2 of the *First Steps* section (<htcite0>) and in the previous text (<htcite1>). We recommend studying both before continuing with this one.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "aa6b052f-05ca-4979-a3a7-a4b7e70618ef",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The Difference of Equals",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we studied the equality between two quantities, we learned that, when the quantities are completely equal, subtracting one from the other yields zero (<htcite0>).\n\nIn some situations, even if we do not have entirely equal objects, we can still consider them to be the same objects (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "aa6b052f-05ca-4979-a3a7-a4b7e70618ef",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The Difference of Equals",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a05ccb5a-182d-4321-8105-f30ee47edfc6",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "I live on the planet...",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Almost the Same</b><br /><br />Can we use subtraction to determine whether two objects are similar?<br /><br />What happens if we compare the same two objects in more than one way — can each comparison give a different answer?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_atom",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Hydrogen Family\n\nWhile measuring the hydrogen atom in <htdate0>, Francis William Aston found that the values he obtained for the mass of the hydrogen atom were greater than expected, even after allowing for possible errors. This suggested that something unexpected was present in his measurements (<htcite0>).\n\n#### Recalling the Hydrogen Atom\n\nWhen we study the hydrogen atom, we learn that it has only one proton in its nucleus and one electron (<htcite1>). Those two parts let us write the atom in fractional form, as shown in **Calculation 1**.\n\nIn **Calculation 1**, the whole atom (1) is written as two halves (2/2): one component is the proton, and the other is the electron. The halves count the atom's two components — they do not mean equal shares of the atom's mass.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7e5a60a0-b19d-49a4-a2c2-fd3467d98ead",
                     "text": "Jolly, W.L.",
                     "page": "hydrogen",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f5d420a5-02eb-4b7e-b318-05327b64a90f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Evolution of knowledge – The hydrogen atom",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1927",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1 + 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: The hydrogen atom (1) split into its two components: the electron (1/2) and the proton (1/2) — the fractions count the components, not equal shares of the atom's mass.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Along with the atom's components, studying the hydrogen atom also teaches us that atoms emit light at very specific wavelengths (<htcite0>), as shown in **Graph 1**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f5d420a5-02eb-4b7e-b318-05327b64a90f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Evolution of knowledge – The hydrogen atom",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewbox=\"0 0 800 200\"><rect width=\"800\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#0f172a\"/><text x=\"400\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#e2e8f0\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Hydrogen Atom Emission Spectrum</text> <g transform=\"translate(50, 80)\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"700\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"0\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">1</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">90</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">130</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">365</text><text x=\"518\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">700</text><text x=\"590\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">820</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\">Wavelength (nm)</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lyman</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#34d399\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Balmer</text><text x=\"600\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c084fc\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Paschen</text><rect x=\"175\" y=\"15\" width=\"350\" height=\"40\" fill=\"url(#visibleGradient)\" opacity=\"0.3\"/> <text x=\"350\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#e2e8f0\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\">Visible light</text><line x1=\"91\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"91\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"93\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"93\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"102\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"102\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <line x1=\"175\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"2.0\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"3.0\"/><line x1=\"185\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"3.0\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"3.0\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/> <line x1=\"224\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"224\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"274\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"274\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"455\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"455\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#22c55e\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/> <line x1=\"600\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"3.0\"/> <line x1=\"605\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"605\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"3.0\"/><line x1=\"609\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"609\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"612\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"612\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"620\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"620\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"630\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"630\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"650\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"650\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"675\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"675\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#c084fc\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><defs><linearGradient id=\"visibleGradient\" x1=\"0%\" y1=\"0%\" x2=\"100%\" y2=\"0%\"><stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#8b5cf6\"/><stop offset=\"20%\" stop-color=\"#3b82f6\"/><stop offset=\"40%\" stop-color=\"#22c55e\"/><stop offset=\"60%\" stop-color=\"#eab308\"/><stop offset=\"80%\" stop-color=\"#f97316\"/><stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#ef4444\"/></linearGradient></defs></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Spectrum of the hydrogen atom.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The spectrum is made up of vertical lines spaced according to the measurements taken.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: What's New?</b><br /><br />After reviewing the hydrogen atom, what do you think Francis William Aston found in his measurements of that atom?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Isotopes\n\nOur understanding of atoms requires that all atoms of the same element have the same number of protons (<htcite0>). Therefore, whatever Aston found in his measurements could not be an extra proton in the nucleus — that would mean a different atom altogether.\n\nAston's measurements were confirmed in <htdate0> by Harold C. Urey, F. G. Brickwedde, and G. M. Murphy. Their measurements showed that hydrogen has an isotope — an atom with the same number of protons but a different number of neutrons — that has, in addition to the proton, a neutron in its nucleus. This isotope is called deuterium (<htcite1>; <htcite2>).\n\nBesides deuterium, in <htdate1> Marcus Laurence Elwin Oliphant, P. Harteck, and Ernest Rutherford bombarded deuterium in an experiment and discovered another hydrogen isotope with two neutrons: tritium (<htcite3>; <htcite4>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19e9bbd7-ef72-462e-993b-70e6bbdc966b",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "atomic number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "9",
                        "day": "6"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3374c9a6-6277-47fc-8fdd-6b29ff5c77b3",
                     "text": "Urey, H. C., Brickwedde, F. G., & Murphy, G. M.",
                     "page": "pp. 164 - 165",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1932",
                        "month": "1",
                        "day": "1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7e5a60a0-b19d-49a4-a2c2-fd3467d98ead",
                     "text": "Jolly, W.L.",
                     "page": "hydrogen",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d3016e0f-57e0-49f4-b801-ea1ab2d312a3",
                     "text": "Marcus Laurence Elwin Oliphant; P. Harteck; Ernest Rutherford, F. R. S.",
                     "page": "pp. 692 - 703",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1934",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "86fbef72-9269-4aa5-8be8-b2209df4478c",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "tritium",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
