      {
         "id": "SECTION_who",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### To Be or Not to Be?\n\nBuilding on the definition of hydrogen and its isotopes, we can now generalize this information to all atoms and determine when we are talking about a specific type of atom.\n\n#### Different Atoms\n\nTo know whether we are dealing with a single type of atom or several types, we compare their atomic numbers — that is, their number of protons (<htcite0>).\n\nFor example, consider the hydrogen atom (H), which has a single proton in its nucleus, and the helium atom (He), which has two.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "19e9bbd7-ef72-462e-993b-70e6bbdc966b",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "atomic number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "9",
                        "day": "6"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Protons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>He</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>H</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Protons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Protons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Comparison between the number of protons in helium and hydrogen atoms.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "**Calculation 2** shows us:\n- **Line 1**: We want to compare the number of protons in a helium atom (He) with that in a hydrogen atom (H).\n- **Line 2**: Helium has 2 protons, while hydrogen has 1.\n- **Line 3**: Consequently, the difference in the number of protons is not zero.\n\nSince the result is not zero, helium and hydrogen are not the same type of atom.\n\n#### The Isotopes\n\nWhen we compare two atoms of the same element, the proton subtraction always gives zero. However, the number of neutrons can vary — and that variation tells us we are dealing with isotopes.\n\nFor instance, a common hydrogen atom has no neutrons in its nucleus; deuterium (D), on the other hand, has one. We can therefore detect isotopes by also comparing the number of neutrons in atoms of the same element, in the following way:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Neutrons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>D</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>H</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Neutrons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">-</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>Total Neutrons</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Comparison between the number of neutrons in hydrogen and deuterium.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Calculation 3**, we can conclude:\n- **Line 1**: We want to compare the number of neutrons in deuterium (D) with that in a common hydrogen atom (H).\n- **Line 2**: Deuterium has 1 neutron, while common hydrogen has 0.\n- **Line 3**: Consequently, the result is not zero.\n\nAs in **Calculation 2**, the non-zero result shows that the nuclei are not identical. Yet this does not make them different chemical elements: since both have the same number of protons, both are still forms of hydrogen — they are isotopes of the same element.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Almost the Same</b><br /><br />Look around you.<br /><br />Can you find something that looks similar to something else, but with slightly different characteristics?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_macro",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Macroscopic Comparisons\n\nIt is not only in the atomic world that things which seem identical turn out, in fact, to be different.\n\nLet's compare the two circles shown in **Table 1** with a practice exercise.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\" style=\"width: 50%;\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Circles</th></tr> <tr><td><div class=\"circle\" style=\"background-color:blue;width:5.5vw;height:5.5vw\"></div></td> <td><i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle\" style=\"color:red;font-size:6vw;\"></i></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Circles of the same diameter, but with different colors.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Are They the Same?</b><br /><br /><b>Graph 1</b> shows the light spectrum of the hydrogen atom. Notice that in the background — in the region of visible light — there are shades of blue on the left and red on the right. Light is ordered by its wavelengths, and each color corresponds to a different wavelength.<br /><br />Blue and red correspond to ranges of wavelengths, not to single fixed values: blue light lies roughly between 450 and 500 nanometers, and red light roughly between 620 and 750 nanometers. As illustrative values within those ranges, we will use approximately 450 nanometers for blue and approximately 700 nanometers for red. With that in mind, we can check whether the two circles in <b>Table 1</b> are the same or not.<br /><br />1. The first step is to measure the diameter of both circles. Place the ruler at one edge of each circle and measure to the opposite edge, then subtract the two results. In this step, you will see that they are the same type of geometric figure — circles of the same size.<br />2. Now, subtract the wavelength associated with the color of one circle from the wavelength associated with the color of the other (Red − Blue = 700 − 450). The result is not zero, so the circles are not the same color.</div></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
